      "math_academy_id": "q-132427",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14161415/assets/019f0f9f83ddf323d543d109677335cb3ae72a200baf834453a0eca16a9353ba.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}6y\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above.",
      "worked_solution": "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14161415/assets/d222e86f6af7c3ed2b35711114c9062d6f0c9b0f2551aa44cac33318c6e5813f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,x+2\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}6y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}6y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}6y\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}6y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3[{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2)}^{2}-{(x+2)}^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3[{(x+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2)}^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{x}^{2}-4x)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-{x}^{2}+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{20}+{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}){|}_{-2}^{0}+(-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{20}-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{3(-2)^{5}}{20}+(-2)^{3}-6(-2)^{2})-\\frac{3(2)^{5}}{20}-(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}+8+24-\\frac{24}{5}-8+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
